%% Aesop’s fable is in the public domain. This retelling is new, and free to copy (CC0). %%

# The North Wind and the Sun

The North Wind and the Sun once argued about which of them was the stronger.

@Wind (booming) “I am,” said the North Wind. “I knock down trees. I push ships across the sea.”

@Sun (mildly) “Strength is not always loud,” said the Sun.

Just then a traveler came along the road, wrapped in a thick wool cloak. [footsteps on gravel]

@Sun “Let us agree,” said the Sun. “Whichever of us can take that cloak off his back is the stronger.”

@Wind “Easy,” said the North Wind. “Watch me.”

{page}

::: @Wind huffing, louder and louder
One puff!
Two puffs!
THREE PUFFS!
:::

[a howling gale]

But the harder the North Wind blew, the tighter the traveler held his cloak.

@Traveler [shivers] “What a day,” he muttered. “What a dreadful, freezing day.”

At last the North Wind gave up. @Wind [a long, tired sigh]

{pause 1s}

{page}

Then the Sun came out from behind a cloud, and simply shone. [birdsong]

@(slowly, letting it warm up) The air grew mild. Then warm. Then hot.

@Traveler (wiping his brow) “Well! What a lovely afternoon.”

And he took off his cloak and sat down in the shade of a tree.

@Sun (gently) “You see? Gentleness often does what force cannot.”

{book only} *Moral: Persuasion is better than force.*

{audio only} And that is the moral of the story: persuasion is better than force.
